.TH NDJSON_FEED 3
.SH NAME
ndjson_feed \- read newline delimited JSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <json.h>
.P
.BI "size_t ndjson_feed(ndjson* " nd ", const char* " x ", size_t " n ", ndjson_callback* " cb ", void* " user ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Newline delimited JSON (application/x-ndjson, JSON Lines) has one JSON value on every line. Servers use it to stream
the tokens of a language model, one object per line, often with a field "done": true in the last one.
.P
.BR ndjson_feed ()
takes
.I n
bytes at
.I x
in any pieces, cut anywhere, collects them into lines and parses every complete line with
.BR json_parse_text (3).
.I cb(value, user)
is called with the tree; it is released when the callback returns, so copy what is needed. Empty lines (and CR LF line ends) are ignored; a line that is not
exactly one valid JSON value is skipped and counted in
.IR nd->errors .
A last line without a newline is not used until the newline comes.
.P
.I nd->lines
counts the values that were handed out.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The number of values passed to cb.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
static void
on_line(jsonval* v, void* user) {
  jsonval t = json_property_path(*v, "message.content");

  if(t.type == JSON_STRING)
    buffer_put(buffer_1, t.stringv.s, t.stringv.len);
}

ndjson nd;
ndjson_init(&nd);
while((n = http_recv(&h, buf, sizeof(buf))) > 0)
  ndjson_feed(&nd, buf, n, on_line, 0);
ndjson_free(&nd);
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
ndjson_init(3), json_parse_text(3), json_property_path(3), sse_feed(3)
